    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/07/hoka-rocket-x-3-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The 40/33 mm dual-layer PEBA midsole provides strong impact protection with a softer, bouncier heel/rearfoot and a slightly firmer, more aggressive forefoot feel; heel transitions are smooth and quick.",
        "Responsiveness: The full-length winged carbon plate plus PEBA foam creates high energy return with a snappy, fast ride that feels better as pace increases, especially for tempo/interval efforts.",
        "Stability: It stands out as a stable-neutral super shoe, using significant medial/lateral sidewalls, a wider midfoot, and a stiff (non-flexible) plated platform for guidance; heel counter is semi/mildly flexible but secure enough for most.",
        "Durability: Durability is a weak point, with reviewers flagging questionable outsole longevity and listing overall durability expectation as average to below average."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/07/hoka-rocket-x-3-multi-tester-review.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The dual-density PEBA midsole at high stack (about 40/33 mm in standard sizing) provides strong impact protection and comfort, with a generally firmer feel, a softer top layer in the forefoot, and a firmer heel structure for control.",
        "Responsiveness: The forked carbon plate with winglets and slight flex delivers a snappy, energetic, and smooth ride, with good energy return that feels less harsh/aggressive than some rival super shoes.",
        "Stability: Testers consistently call it very stable for a 40 mm racer, supported by raised medial sidewalls, a broad platform, subtle plate integration, a stout heel counter, and secure lateral/ankle lockdown.",
        "Durability: The sticky rubber outsole, reported high build quality, and low-moisture fast-drying upper indicate good material longevity, though no long-term outsole wear data is provided in the text."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/hoka-rocket-x-3-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The 40/33 mm stack and dual-density PEBA provide strong impact protection with a softer, more forgiving ride, and the forefoot feels notably softer than prior versions.",
        "Responsiveness: Updated foam placement around the carbon plate gives a snappier, more energetic toe-off with clear pop, but overall feel is more smooth/controlled than ultra-aggressive.",
        "Stability: Firmer foam under the plate, a more structured padded heel counter, and secure lace/upper lockdown produce solid stability with good lateral control, especially for heel strikers.",
        "Durability: The rubber outsole has strong grip and coverage, looked solid after ~40 miles, and was described as robust for super-shoe longevity."
      ]
    }
  ],
